document: docs/design/0002-document-authoring-contracts.md
blob: dce2978689dce385df0c662a3c74f70dee4c7e84
decision: pass
reviewer: claude-opus-5-5 (self-review)
audience: Reviewers and implementers
purpose: How should documents be classified, stored, written and checked so each type answers its reader question in a fixed order?

Proposal (draft) to classify documents by the reader's decision and task, fix a required body order and content per type (decision-record through runbook), reorganize public-ai-qa folders into shared documents, code-adjacent guides and dated reports, and enforce it through structure checks, editorial review and hash-matched review receipts.
Translated from Korean to English across all six sections and the sources list; tables, the docs tree, type subsections and the transition steps keep their rows and order. Dates and counts (2026-09-08; 2/2/17/9 documents; 3-sentence summary and 4-sentence paragraph targets; the 10-run example) are unchanged; frontmatter is byte-identical.
Korean kept on purpose in section 3 (Style and wording): the sentence endings (~한다, ~이다, ~를 확인했다, ~를 권고한다, ~는 확인이 필요하다), the causation and heading wording examples, and the before/after example table, each with an English gloss, because their point is Korean prose style. Also kept 해당 없음 in the omission rule with a gloss.
Per-type "required order" labels were translated into English because the document describes section roles, not literal headings, and document-types.yaml matches section questions by pattern rather than fixed heading text.
Tree comments inside the code block were translated as prose; paths, identifiers and link targets unchanged.
Deferred in .engsys/project.yaml; docs check reports 0 findings and link check passes. Self-review by the translating agent.

